>
	<Fragment slot="intro">
		<p>
			eGFR stands for estimated glomerular filtration rate. It is a number worked out from a routine
			blood test that shows how well your kidneys filter waste. The lab measures creatinine in your
			blood, then uses it with your age and sex in an equation to produce the result. A normal adult
			eGFR is usually above 90.
		</p>
	</Fragment>

	<h2>What does GFR mean?</h2>
	<p>
		Glomerular filtration rate is the volume of blood your kidneys filter each minute, adjusted to a
		standard body size. The glomeruli are the tiny filters in each kidney. GFR is widely used as the
		overall measure of kidney function.
	</p>

	<h2>eGFR vs GFR</h2>
	<p>
		True GFR can only be measured with a clearance test, in which a substance is injected and timed as
		it leaves the blood. That is accurate but slow and invasive, so it is rarely used. eGFR estimates
		the same value from a simple blood test, which is why it is on most lab reports. Your doctor may
		ask for a measured GFR when getting the number exactly right would change your care.
	</p>

	<h2>How the test works</h2>
	<ol>
		<li>A sample of blood is taken from your arm. Creatinine is often part of a basic or comprehensive metabolic panel.</li>
		<li>The lab measures your serum creatinine, and sometimes cystatin C.</li>
		<li>An equation, usually CKD-EPI 2021 in the US, converts that value into an eGFR.</li>
	</ol>
	<p>
		You can run the same calculation yourself with our <a href="/">GFR calculator</a> if your report
		lists only creatinine. Follow your lab's instructions on preparation, because other tests in the
		same panel may require fasting.
	</p>

	<h2>What is a normal eGFR?</h2>
	<p>
		A normal eGFR for adults is usually above 90 mL/min/1.73m². Averages fall with age, from about 116
		in your 20s to about 75 at age 70 and over. See the full
		<a href="/normal-gfr-by-age">normal GFR by age chart</a>. Some labs simply report "&gt;60" or "&gt;90"
		rather than an exact figure. To understand a specific value, see
		<a href="/egfr-results">what your eGFR number means</a>.
	</p>

	<h2>Who should get an eGFR test?</h2>
	<p>Kidney disease often causes no symptoms early on, so testing is based on risk. It is especially relevant if you have:</p>
	<ul>
		<li>Diabetes</li>
		<li>High blood pressure</li>
		<li>Heart disease</li>
		<li>A family history of kidney failure</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		It is usually paired with a urine albumin test, because kidney damage can show up in the urine
		while eGFR is still normal.
	</p>

	<h2>What can affect your result</h2>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>Can lower eGFR:</strong> a lot of cooked meat before the test, creatine supplements, intense exercise, dehydration, high muscle mass, and certain medicines.</li>
		<li><strong>Can raise eGFR:</strong> a vegan or vegetarian diet, low muscle mass, and pregnancy.</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		Because of these effects, one result is not a diagnosis. CKD requires changes that last more than
		three months. If muscle mass or diet may be skewing your result, ask about a
		<a href="/cystatin-c-gfr-calculator">cystatin C test</a>.
	</p>

